    "overview": "Analyze the structural sectoral composition of the Indian economy and its unique bypass of traditional industrialization.",
    "summary": "A nation's economy is traditionally classified into three primary sectors: <strong>Primary</strong> (agriculture, forestry, mining), <strong>Secondary</strong> (manufacturing, construction, electricity), and <strong>Tertiary</strong> (services, banking, IT, trade). Unlike Western and East Asian economies that transitioned sequentially from agriculture to manufacturing and then to services (Kuznets' hypothesis), India experienced a unique <strong>'structural transformation leap'</strong>, moving directly from agriculture to a dominant <strong>services-led economy</strong> (~54% of Gross Value Added) while agriculture continues to employ ~45% of the total workforce.",
    "fullNotes": "<h3>1. Sectoral Architecture of the Indian Economy</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1. Primary Sector (Agriculture & Allied Activities):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Includes crop cultivation, livestock/dairy, forestry, fishing, and mining/quarrying.</li>\n      <li>Contributes approximately <strong>17%–18% of India's GVA</strong>, but absorbs nearly <strong>45%–46% of the national workforce</strong> (Periodic Labour Force Survey - PLFS).</li>\n      <li>Characterized by acute <strong>disguised unemployment</strong> (marginal productivity of labor is zero or near-zero) and low capital formation.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Secondary Sector (Industry & Manufacturing):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Includes manufacturing (registered and unregistered), construction, electricity, gas, water supply, and other utility services.</li>\n      <li>Contributes approximately <strong>27%–29% of India's GVA</strong>, employing around 25% of the labor force.</li>\n      <li>Identified as the critical 'missing middle'; initiatives like <em>Make in India</em> and Production Linked Incentive (PLI) schemes aim to raise manufacturing's share to 25% of GDP.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Tertiary Sector (Services Sector):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Includes trade, hotels, transport, communication, storage, financial services, real estate, professional services, public administration, and defense.</li>\n      <li>The supreme growth engine of modern India, contributing approximately <strong>53%–55% of national GVA</strong> while employing ~30% of the workforce.</li>\n      <li>Driven by high-productivity software exports (IT-BPM), telecom, financial services, and e-commerce.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Quaternary & Quinary Sectors:</strong> Knowledge-based services (R&D, advanced computing, data analytics) and top-level executive decision-making (policy formulation, government leaders).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. The Indian Structural Transformation Anomaly</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Classical Economic Model (Colin Clark & Simon Kuznets):</strong> Economic development typically shifts labor and output progressively: Agriculture &rarr; Manufacturing &rarr; Services.</li>\n  <li><strong>India's Distinct Path:</strong> Agriculture's output share declined rapidly from ~55% in 1950 to ~18% today; however, the secondary industrial sector remained stagnant around 25%–28%, with the <strong>Services sector jumping directly from ~30% to over 54%</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Employment-Output Mismatch:</strong> While services produce 54% of income, they employ only 30% of workers (due to high capital and skill intensity), leaving nearly half the population dependent on low-yield agriculture.</li>\n</ul>",
