    "diagram": "diag-econ-national-income-flow.svg",
    "topicId": "top-econ-01-03",
    "subjectId": "gk-economy",
    "introduction": "Master the distinction between Real and Nominal economic growth, the GDP Deflator price index, and official NSO revisions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Nominal GDP vs Real GDP</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Nominal GDP (GDP at Current Prices):</strong> The market value of all final goods and services produced in a year, evaluated at the prices prevailing in that <strong>same year</strong>. It reflects both changes in physical production volume AND changes in general price levels (inflation).</li>\n  <li><strong>Real GDP (GDP at Constant Prices):</strong> The value of final goods and services produced in a year, evaluated at fixed prices of a chosen <strong>Base Year</strong>.\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Significance:</em> Real GDP eliminates the distorting effect of inflation; an increase in Real GDP reflects an <strong>actual physical expansion in goods and services produced</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Official Growth Rate:</em> When the Government or RBI reports India's GDP growth rate (e.g., 7.2%), it refers strictly to <strong>Real GDP growth</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The GDP Deflator (Implicit Price Deflator):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>A measure of the general level of prices of all new, domestically produced, final goods and services in an economy.</li>\n      <li><em>Formula:</em> <strong>GDP Deflator = [Nominal GDP / Real GDP] &times; 100</strong></li>\n      <li><em>Advantage over CPI/WPI:</em> Unlike CPI or WPI which track a fixed basket of consumer/wholesale goods, the GDP Deflator covers the <strong>entire spectrum of domestically produced goods and services</strong>, and its basket updates automatically every year.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. National Statistical Office (NSO) & Base Year Methodology</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Nodal Agency:</strong> National Income statistics are compiled and released by the <strong>National Statistical Office (NSO)</strong> under the <strong>Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI)</strong> (formed in 2019 by merging the Central Statistics Office - CSO and National Sample Survey Office - NSSO).</li>\n  <li><strong>Current Base Year:</strong> The current base year for India's national accounts and GDP calculation is <strong>2011–12</strong> (revised in January 2015 from the earlier base year of 2004–05).</li>\n  <li><strong>Key Methodological Changes Introduced in 2015 Revision:</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li><strong>Headline Growth Metric:</strong> Headline growth was shifted from <em>GDP at Factor Cost</em> to <strong>GDP at Constant Market Prices</strong> (conforming to international IMF / UN System of National Accounts - SNA 2008 standards).</li>\n      <li><strong>Sectoral Growth:</strong> Sectoral economic performance measured using <strong>Gross Value Added (GVA) at Basic Prices</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Enterprise Database:</strong> Shifted from factory-level Annual Survey of Industries (ASI) to the Ministry of Corporate Affairs <strong>MCA-21 electronic filing database</strong>, capturing headquarters value addition and services uncaptured earlier.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>",
