    "topicId": "top-econ-02-02",
    "subjectId": "gk-economy",
    "introduction": "Analyze the second wave of planning, poverty eradication campaigns, rolling plans, and structural high-growth periods.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Middle Planning Phase (4th to 7th Plans)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Fourth Five-Year Plan (1969–1974):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Objectives:</em> Growth with stability and progressive achievement of self-reliance (formulated under D.R. Gadgil; introduced the <strong>Gadgil Formula</strong> for central plan assistance to states).</li>\n      <li><em>Milestones:</em> <strong>Nationalisation of 14 major commercial banks (July 1969)</strong>, the 1971 Indo-Pak War leading to the liberation of Bangladesh, severe oil shock (1973), and the <strong>Pokhran-I Nuclear Test ('Smiling Buddha', May 1974)</strong>. Target: 5.7%, Actual: 3.3%.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Fifth Five-Year Plan (1974–1979):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Drafted by:</em> D.D. Dhar.</li>\n      <li><em>Slogans & Mandate:</em> <strong>'Garibi Hatao' (Removal of Poverty)</strong> and achievement of self-reliance. Launched the <strong>Twenty Point Programme (1975)</strong> and Minimum Needs Programme (MNP).</li>\n      <li><em>Termination:</em> The newly elected Janata Party government terminated the 5th Plan in 1978, one year ahead of schedule (ran from 1974 to 1978). Achieved 4.8% growth against a 4.4% target.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Rolling Plan (1978–1980):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Concept proposed by Swedish economist <strong>Gunnar Myrdal</strong> in his book <em>'Asian Drama'</em>. Introduced in India by the Janata Government under D.T. Lakdawala.</li>\n      <li>A rolling plan has three parts: (1) an annual budget, (2) a medium-term plan for 3–5 years that updates every year, and (3) a long-term perspective plan for 10–20 years. Terminated when Indira Gandhi returned to power in 1980.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Sixth Five-Year Plan (1980–1985):</strong> Marked the beginning of economic deregulation. Nationalisation of 6 more commercial banks (1980). Focused on poverty alleviation through programs like <strong>IRDP, NREP, and TRYSEM</strong>. Established <strong>NABARD (1982)</strong> and <strong>EXIM Bank (1982)</strong>. Achieved 5.7% growth against 5.2% target.</li>\n  <li><strong>Seventh Five-Year Plan (1985–1990):</strong> Focused on 'Food, Work, and Productivity' under Rajiv Gandhi. Expansion of electronics, telecommunications, and IT industries. Jawahar Rozgar Yojana (JRY) launched in 1989. Succeeded with 6.0% growth against a 5.0% target. Followed by <strong>Annual Plans (1990–1992)</strong> due to acute economic and political crisis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Post-Reform Planning (8th to 12th Plans)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Eighth Five-Year Plan (1992–1997):</strong> The first post-reform plan under Prime Minister P.V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh (John W. Miller model). Prioritized human resource development, health, and education. Achieved <strong>6.8% growth against a target of 5.6%</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ninth Five-Year Plan (1997–2002):</strong> Focused on <em>'Growth with Social Justice and Equity'</em> under Atal Bihari Vajpayee. Hit by East Asian Financial Crisis (1997) and Pokhran-II sanctions (1998). Achieved 5.4% against 6.5% target.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tenth Five-Year Plan (2002–2007):</strong> Aimed at doubling per capita income in 10 years and reducing poverty by 5 percentage points. High economic boom; achieved <strong>7.6% growth</strong> against 8.0% target.</li>\n  <li><strong>Eleventh Five-Year Plan (2007–2012):</strong> Theme: <em>'Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth'</em> under Dr. Manmohan Singh. Achieved highest average decadal growth of <strong>8.0%</strong> despite the 2008 Global Financial Crisis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Twelfth Five-Year Plan (2012–2017):</strong> Theme: <em>'Faster, More Inclusive and Sustainable Growth'</em>. Targeted 8% growth. Formally the <strong>final Five-Year Plan of India</strong>, as the Government of India abolished the Planning Commission in 2014 and established <strong>NITI Aayog on January 1, 2015</strong>, ending the five-year planning era.</li>\n</ul>",
