    "introduction": "Master the statutory regulatory architecture of Indian capital markets, stock exchanges, insurance, and pensions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Capital Market & Stock Exchanges of India</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Primary Market vs Secondary Market:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Primary Market (New Issues Market):</em> Companies raise fresh equity capital directly from the public through <strong>Initial Public Offerings (IPOs)</strong>, Further Public Offerings (FPOs), or Rights Issues. The proceeds go directly to the issuing company.</li>\n      <li><em>Secondary Market (Stock Exchange):</em> Platform for trading previously issued securities between investors. Provides liquidity to investors without directly funding the company.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Established in <strong>1875</strong> by Premchand Roychand as 'The Native Share & Stock Brokers' Association' on Dalal Street, Mumbai. Recognized as <strong>Asia's oldest stock exchange</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Benchmark Index:</em> <strong>BSE SENSEX (Sensitivity Index)</strong>, introduced in 1986 with base year 1978–79 = 100. Composed of <strong>30 large, liquid, representative blue-chip stocks</strong> based on free-float market capitalization.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>National Stock Exchange (NSE):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Incorporated in <strong>1992</strong> on the recommendation of the <strong>Pherwani Committee</strong>; commenced operations in 1994. Pioneered electronic screen-based nationwide trading, dematerialization, and derivatives in India. Recognized as the world's largest derivatives exchange by contract volume.</li>\n      <li><em>Benchmark Index:</em> <strong>NIFTY 50</strong>, introduced in 1996 with base year 1995 = 1000. Composed of <strong>50 premier diversified Indian stocks</strong> across key economic sectors.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Dematerialisation & Depositories:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Under the Depositories Act, 1996, physical paper shares were converted into electronic book-entry balances (Demat Account).</li>\n      <li>Two national depositories: <strong>National Securities Depository Limited (NSDL, 1996)</strong> and <strong>Central Depository Services Limited (CDSL, 1999)</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Settlement cycle: India successfully transitioned to the lightning-fast <strong>T+1 (and optional T+0) settlement cycle</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Major Financial Regulatory Authorities</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1. Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Established in 1988 as a non-statutory body; granted statutory autonomy on <strong>January 30, 1992</strong> under the <strong>SEBI Act, 1992</strong> (prompted by the 1992 securities market scam). Headquartered in Mumbai.</li>\n      <li><em>Tripartite Mandate:</em> (1) Protecting the interests of investors in securities, (2) Promoting the development of securities markets, and (3) Regulating the securities market.</li>\n      <li><em>Powers:</em> Quasi-legislative (drafts regulations), quasi-executive (conducts investigations/audits), and quasi-judicial (issues cease-and-desist orders and penalties). Appeals against SEBI orders lie before the <strong>Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)</strong> under Section 15T.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Statutory body constituted under the <strong>IRDA Act, 1999</strong> on the recommendation of the <strong>Malhotra Committee (1994)</strong>. Headquartered in <strong>Hyderabad</strong> (Telangana). Regulates and promotes life and general insurance industries.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority (PFRDA):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Established under the <strong>PFRDA Act, 2013</strong> (notified in 2014; operated since 2003 under executive resolution). Headquartered in New Delhi. Regulates and administers the <strong>National Pension System (NPS)</strong> and Atal Pension Yojana (APY).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>4. Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Non-statutory apex council set up in 2010 on Raghuram Rajan Committee recommendation; chaired by the <strong>Union Finance Minister</strong>, comprising heads of all financial regulators (RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA, IBBI) to oversee macro-prudential stability and inter-regulatory coordination.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>",
